{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean pushed head 57ad2be0d3f7d10010196e1e91ddf5cf84b0e72b, signing all three artifacts: .gitseq bfc1b4c9, cmd/gs 3e198c7c, and docs 6768dd66.\n\nNot on a security defect. I found no exploitable one, the guards are unusually well thought out, and the locking design is the strongest part of the change. The objection is that two conditions of satisfaction you wrote yourself are unmet.\n\nYour first condition was to verify by mutation that removing each guard makes its test fail. Twelve mutations were applied, each confirmed by diff and confirmed to compile before measurement, so there are no non-measurements in the set. Six survive. Four sit on guards you or the design note name.\n\nThe one that matters is publication.go 629, the fields[1] != ref clause in remoteBranchHead. That clause is the post-push remote proof, which is the entire security premise of the feature, and nothing in the suite defends it. I reproduced the attack myself rather than taking it from my reviewer. git ls-remote matches on the tail of a ref name, not exactly: I created a bare remote whose only ref is refs/heads/evil/refs/heads/main, queried it with the exact command the code runs, and got exit 0 with the attacker's OID and ref. With that clause removed, a remote that has no refs/heads/main but does have refs/heads/evil/refs/heads/main hands gs publish an OID it accepts as head; the tool then reads .gitseq and the watch globs from the attacker's tree and signs artifact records naming that commit, while the report says refs/heads/main. That is a hostile remote making it sign something false, which is the first item on your own security list. The guard as written closes it and is correct. The defect is that a future refactor can delete it with a green suite.\n\nThree more survivors bear directly on your conditions. Removing the validatePublicationBatchSize call site in runPublication changes nothing observable, so the 256-path ceiling documented in limits.md is unwired-testable while its pure function is tested. Blanking the IdempotencyKey survives, and that key is the only guard against a crash between submit and save double-appending, since the fold requires only path and commit present and does no duplicate check. And removing the !published filter in unpublishedPaths survives, which matters more than it looks: merge succession and publish use different idempotency namespaces, so that filter is the only thing stopping publish from filing a second artifact at a path and commit a merge already published, and this head's own .gitseq watches notes/**.md where merge publishes routinely.\n\nYour second unmet condition is that every row of the hostile-input table has a decided outcome and a test. The over-ceiling row is decided in runPublication and tested only at the pure function.\n\nOne more thing I would want fixed before this becomes the default publication path, and it is availability rather than integrity. A permanently failed outbox entry is retained forever by design, correctly, but a batch is pruned only when every entry is done, so the batch is retained too. That actor's later runs are then refused before reaching the remote, and every other actor is refused by refuseForeignPublicationBatches. An actor retired while an entry is pending, or simply an actor whose session ends with an entry unresolved, wedges publication for the whole repository. There is no --clear or --abandon and publish.md documents no recovery; the only remedy is hand-deleting the outbox file, which destroys the surfaced failure the design deliberately kept. The fail-closed direction is right; the recovery half is missing.\n\nTwo smaller findings, reported without asking for changes. Path validation refuses invalid UTF-8, control bytes, commas and oversize, but not .. components; a crafted tree carrying notes/.. passes every refusal and is signed. I traced the consumers and found no filesystem use of an artifact path, so I could construct only a nonsense record and not an exploit, but you named that input explicitly and today the answer is that it is published rather than refused. And publicationDecision does not check that the resident's answer names the event it asked about; the local fallback gets this right by construction, ValidateURL confines --server to loopback, and the honest resident keys correctly, so it is not exploitable today, but it is one comparison on the call that decides whether a signed artifact is marked done.\n\nArchitecture: preserved. The change is confined to the cmd/gs outer surface - new publication.go and the two lock files, one case in main.go - and touches nothing under kernel, gitstore, workroom, nexus or apphost. docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in this same head with a precise statement that it adds no file meaning to the kernel and no Git or outbox access to the fold, which matches the code, and limits.md gains the Published paths table.\n\nSecurity, item by item against your list: post-push remote proof correct but unpinned; process identity correct and pinned, no git-config fallback; linked-worktree and cross-process locking correct and the best part of the change, lock at the common dir so it is per-repository, flock and LockFileEx, OS-released on death, and no os.SameFile anywhere - savePublicationJSON is CreateTemp, Chmod, Sync, Rename, directory Sync, so there is no identity to judge; actor handoff correct with the frontier repository-owned; crash recovery fails in the safe direction, queueing fsynced before the first submission; absence is pending and never a verdict; failure continuation correct and tested; validation before signing correct in the important direction, since loadPublicationOutbox re-validates on every read so a tampered pending entry is refused before a signature exists. Bounds are declared and documented, with the soft spot that the 4 MiB outbox limit is enforced after os.ReadFile has already read the file.\n\nSimplification: little fluff, and I am not inventing a request. Two observations. Ordering unpublishedPaths before validatePublicationBatchSize would make the ceiling apply to work actually to be done, so a head with 257 watched paths of which 200 are published is not permanently refused. And publicationAfterQueue and publicationAfterLoad are test-only mutable globals in production code, justified in comments and race-clean; noted so the choice is visible rather than as a change.\n\nGates at the exact head, all exit zero: gofmt, git diff --check, go vet, the focused publication tests under -race, go test ./... -count=1 with no failures, and the docset gate run uncached, which executes publish.md's shell block end to end against a real bare remote. merge-tree against main is clean. The Rests-On trailer names c097b25a and all three artifacts rest on your promise rather than the request.\n\nOn the artifact paths, which you asked me to report either way. hugh ratified 0a111591 an hour ago: documentation artifacts belong at the specific file path and a merge publishes its successor there. .gitseq and cmd/gs are specific; docs is the bare path that ruling was about. I am not asking you to change it, because it would not help: planSuccession collects ancestor-directory artifacts and widest picks the widest, so the live bare-docs artifact wins whatever this head publishes, which is the attractor the proposal itself diagnoses. The ruling cannot be honoured by any choice available inside this head, and the honest follow-up is a child request implementing it in cmd/gs/succession.go.\n\nWhat I did not establish: whether the first run's 24-artifact batch is fast enough without --server, since I did not measure a Snapshot at this depth and will not quote a number I did not take; whether a hostile tree with .. survives a push to a real forge, since receive.fsckObjects may reject it; and Windows locking, which I read but did not compile or run.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:bfc1b4c985b469b7634378c60812f4b7d6115611","head":"57ad2be0d3f7d10010196e1e91ddf5cf84b0e72b","verdict":"changes-requested"}}